# ModSecurity, switched on at http level and inherited by every server block
# (spec §11.9).
#
# This file is the whole nginx-side footprint of the WAF, and that is
# deliberate. The alternative — a `modsecurity on;` inside each site's server
# block — would mean editing `site.conf.j2` and re-rendering every vhost to
# turn one site's WAF on, and a site whose owner had hand-edited their vhost
# could not be governed at all. Instead the engine is loaded once here, in
# DetectionOnly, and each site's real policy is a phase-1 rule keyed on its own
# hostnames inside {{ rules_file }}. Turning a site on or off re-renders one
# file and reloads nginx.
#
# `modsecurity_rules_file` is parsed by nginx at configuration load, so a
# broken rules file fails `nginx -t` and the config engine rolls the whole
# change back before anything reloads (spec §10.4 rule 4).
modsecurity on;
modsecurity_rules_file {{ rules_file }};
